course: dictionary
lesson: 8584
title: Scientifically: thinking and working like a scientist
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Scientifically» is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done or known. It comes from the adjective «scientific», which means connected with science, with «-ally» added to make an adverb.
explain: «The first dinosaur to be scientifically named was Megalosaurus, in England in 1824.» Here «scientifically» tells you the naming was done in a careful, science-based way. Test your meaning by swapping it in: «named using scientific methods» still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: In science lessons, «work scientifically» means asking questions, predicting, doing fair tests, measuring, recording and drawing conclusions. Adverts sometimes use «scientifically proven», so always ask what the evidence is.
fact: «Scientifically» is an adverb made from the adjective «scientific» with «-ally» added.
fact: «Scientifically» means in a way that uses science, with careful observing, testing and measuring.
fact: «Scientifically» can also mean according to science, as science shows.
fact: «Work scientifically» is a UK science lesson phrase: asking questions, predicting, doing fair tests, measuring, recording and drawing conclusions.
fact: Adverts sometimes say «scientifically proven», so check whether real evidence is given before you believe the claim.
exercise: «The first dinosaur to be scientifically named was Megalosaurus, in England in 1824.» What does scientifically mean here? | *In a way that follows science and its careful methods | With a loud, cheerful voice | Very slowly, like a sleepy dinosaur | In a hurry, without thinking
exercise: Which action shows someone working scientifically in a science lesson? | Asking nobody any questions | Guessing the answer without looking | Changing everything at once and not writing it down | *Testing one idea at a time and measuring the results
exercise: Scientifically is an adverb. Which word is it made from? | *scientific | scientist | science | scientism
test: An advert says a cream is «scientifically proven». What should a careful reader do? | Buy it because the box is shiny | Ignore all science forever | *Ask what evidence there is and check it | Believe it straight away
test: Which sentence uses «scientifically» correctly? | He ate his lunch scientifically and quickly | She sang scientifically to the radio | The cat sat scientifically on the mat | *The team tested the new seed scientifically, measuring its growth each day
exam: Which phrase from a science lesson means asking questions, predicting, doing fair tests and drawing conclusions? | *work scientifically | scientific guess | scientifically named | sing loudly
